### Phase 2: Current Architecture Map

- [x] Map app routes, feature modules, hooks, services, Supabase tables, and tests.
- [x] Mark shared code candidates.
- [x] Mark risky legacy/demo areas.
- [x] Mark deployment/runtime assumptions.

Architecture inventory:

- App Router surface: 71 page files and 10 API route handlers under `app`.
- Primary app shell: `app/layout.tsx`, `app/providers.tsx`, `app/app/layout.tsx`, `app/app/AppLayoutClient.tsx`, and `src/app-shell`.
- Feature surface: 31 first-level feature folders under `src/features`.
- Largest feature areas by file count: scheduling, inventory, messages, forms, employees, analytics, company updates, tasks, onboarding, system, learning, goals, admin, operations, and gamification.
- Shared/global hooks: 79 files under `src/hooks`, plus feature-local hooks.
- Data access surface: feature repositories, root repositories, services, server helpers, Supabase client/admin helpers, and direct hooks.
- Supabase surface: migrations, generated database types, edge functions, seed files, SQL tests, and remote/local CI gates.
- Test/release surface: Supabase SQL security tests, Playwright specs, visible-module smoke script, onboarding E2E, typecheck scopes, release gates, and deploy-readiness workflow.

Shared code candidates:

- Tenant/auth/permission contracts currently spread across `src/hooks`, `src/app-shell`, `src/lib/auth`, `src/lib/permissions`, and role/permission features.
- Query keys and Supabase error handling should converge around `src/lib/queryKeys.ts`, feature repositories, and shared result/error contracts.
- App shell, navigation, route guards, loading states, and tenant setup state should become the shared shell boundary.
- Inventory, scheduling, tasks, employees, messages, forms, analytics, and company updates are the first module candidates for normalized repository/service conventions.
- Supabase generated types and app-level types need a clear import boundary before any package extraction.

Risky legacy/demo areas:

- `docs/archive` includes historical cleanup, migration, and Capacitor artifacts; these are reference-only.
- `docs/archive/capacitor.config.ts` points to an old Lovable build and must not be reused as the v1 mobile shell.
- Demo/internal routes such as permission demo, add section, custom section routing, dev detectors, and broad gamification/learning/performance areas should not drive pilot navigation.
- `src/mock`, `src/devtools`, performance mocks, financial demo data, and dev detector code must stay out of production claims unless explicitly wired and tested.
- Duplicate route aliases exist between older flat routes and newer `/app/inventory/*` style routes; navigation ownership must be clarified before mobile shell work.

Deployment/runtime assumptions:

- Node 22 in GitHub Actions.
- npm and `package-lock.json` remain the install contract.
- Next.js remains the web/server runtime.
- Managed Supabase remains the backend, with local Supabase used by release gates.
- CI currently expects Supabase CLI, local database reset, security tests, typechecks, production build, onboarding E2E, and visible-module smoke.
- Deploy readiness expects remote Supabase secrets and remote migration drift/contract checks.

Acceptance:

- We can see what exists before restructuring.

Verification:

- Module map references real file paths.

